{#- modules/verifiers/verifiers.j2
    Verifiers: what they are, the four kinds, how their gaps cover each
    other, and why an ensemble of fast, diverse verifiers beats one oracle.

    The four-type taxonomy (software / machine learning / LLM-as-judge /
    human) replaces the older two-type "rules vs. models" split. Four is
    the better frame here because each type fails in a *different* way,
    which is precisely the independence the Weaver result requires — so
    the taxonomy earns the research slide rather than merely preceding it.

    Research anchor: Saad-Falcon et al., "Shrinking the Generation-
    Verification Gap with Weak Verifiers" (Weaver), arXiv:2506.18203.
    Evidence figure: modules/verifiers/img/weaver-weighted-vs-naive-ensembles.png

    Written against the brand-pack primitive contract (see AGENTS.md): no hex
    colors, no brand class names. Import WITH CONTEXT:

      {%- from "modules/verifiers/verifiers.j2" import
          verifiers_are_guardrails, four_kinds, coverage,
          complementarity, ensembling_fast_verifiers,
          weaver_evidence with context %}
-#}

{%- from "modules/branding/" ~ branding ~ "/primitives.j2" import
    content_slide,
    slide_heading,
    card_grid,
    pros_cons,
    matrix,
    quote,
    callout
-%}


{%- macro verifiers_are_king() -%}
{#- Epigraph for the verifiers section. Someone else making the claim
    lands better than the speaker making it, and it names the shift the
    rest of the section explains: the hard part moved from generation to
    verification. -#}
{{ quote("In the Land of AI Agents, the Verifiers Are King",
         "Tariq Shaukat, CEO of Sonar",
         source="AI Engineer World&rsquo;s Fair, 2026",
         url="https://youtu.be/VrpEyglYgeU") }}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro verifiers_are_guardrails() -%}
{#- Definitional slide, and it has to stay. This room arrives knowing the
    word "guardrail" and not the word "verifier"; everything downstream
    depends on that bridge being built explicitly. -#}
{%- call content_slide("Verifiers") %}
  {{ slide_heading("",
                   "What is a guardrail?",
                   "A verifier checks work it did not produce, and can say no.") }}
  {{ card_grid([
    {"title": "The guardrail is the policy",
     "body": "&ldquo;Secrets never land in a repository.&rdquo; That is the rule you want true. It does not check anything by itself.",
     "accent": "info"},
    {"title": "The verifier is what checks",
     "body": "The scanner that reads the diff and answers yes or no. Swap it for a better one and the guardrail is unchanged.",
     "accent": "primary"},
    {"title": "Why it matters",
     "body": "You have far fewer policies than you have ways to check them. Most disagreements about guardrails are really disagreements about verifiers.",
     "accent": "success"},
  ], cols=3) }}
  {{ callout("Paved roads start the agent on the right path.
              <strong>Verifiers are how you know it stayed there.</strong>", accent="primary") }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro four_kinds() -%}
{#- A 2x2 of four distinct things, not a sequence. An arrow-joined pipe
    implied a progression from software to humans, which is wrong: these
    are four different kinds, and the point of the next slide is that no
    ordering among them exists. -#}
{%- call content_slide("Four kinds of verifier") %}
  {{ slide_heading("", "Four kinds of verifier") }}
  {{ card_grid([
    {"title": "Software",
     "body": "Tests, linters, type checkers, schema validation, policy engines. Deterministic.",
     "accent": "primary"},
    {"title": "Machine learning",
     "body": "Classifiers, clustering, anomaly detection. Trained, not prompted.",
     "accent": "info"},
    {"title": "LLM as judge",
     "body": "A model reading the change and arguing about it.",
     "accent": "secondary"},
    {"title": "Human",
     "body": "A reviewer who understands why the system exists.",
     "accent": "warning"},
  ], cols=2, size="lg") }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro coverage() -%}
{#- The centrepiece. A matrix rather than parallel pros/cons lists because
    complementarity is a relationship *between* columns, and lists have no
    way to express one. Read down a column: every kind has dark cells. Read
    across a row: every row has a bright one. -#}
{%- call content_slide("Coverage") %}
  {{ slide_heading("",
                   "Everything is a tradeoff") }}
  {{ matrix(
       headers=[
         {"label": "Software",         "sub": "deterministic"},
         {"label": "Machine learning", "sub": "probabilistic"},
         {"label": "LLM as judge",     "sub": "generative"},
         {"label": "Human",            "sub": "judgement"},
       ],
       rows=[
         {"label": "Catches what nobody anticipated",
          "cells": [
            {"level": "weak",    "text": "only what you wrote"},
            {"level": "partial", "text": "statistical outliers"},
            {"level": "strong",  "text": "unseen patterns"},
            {"level": "strong",  "text": "the truly novel"},
          ]},
         {"label": "Same verdict every time",
          "cells": [
            {"level": "strong",  "text": "by construction"},
            {"level": "partial", "text": "threshold &amp; drift"},
            {"level": "weak",    "text": "varies per run"},
            {"level": "weak",    "text": "varies per mood"},
          ]},
         {"label": "Cheap enough for every change",
          "cells": [
            {"level": "strong",  "text": "milliseconds"},
            {"level": "strong",  "text": "milliseconds"},
            {"level": "partial", "text": "seconds, per call"},
            {"level": "weak",    "text": "the scarce one"},
          ]},
         {"label": "Produces auditable evidence",
          "cells": [
            {"level": "strong",  "text": "the rule and the run"},
            {"level": "weak",    "text": "&ldquo;scored 0.87&rdquo;"},
            {"level": "partial", "text": "explains, may confabulate"},
            {"level": "strong",  "text": "a named approver"},
          ]},
         {"label": "Reads intent across context",
          "cells": [
            {"level": "weak",    "text": "no notion of why"},
            {"level": "weak",    "text": "features, not meaning"},
            {"level": "strong",  "text": "spans files"},
            {"level": "strong",  "text": "knows the business"},
          ]},
       ],
       caption="● strong &nbsp;·&nbsp; ◐ partial &nbsp;·&nbsp; ○ weak") }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro complementarity() -%}
{#- Names the offsetting pairs out loud, then converts the observation into
    the design rule. This replaces the old two-type "models advise, rules
    decide" slide: with four kinds the question is no longer which of two
    wins, but which one is allowed to hold the gate. -#}
{%- call content_slide("How they cover each other") %}
  {{ slide_heading("",
                   "Each kind covers another&rsquo;s blind spot",
                   "Which is the argument for running more than one.") }}
  {{ card_grid([
    {"title": "Software ↔ LLM judge",
     "body": "Near-perfect inverses. Software cannot see what you did not anticipate; the judge cannot promise the same answer twice. Each one&rsquo;s blind spot is the other&rsquo;s strength.",
     "accent": "primary"},
    {"title": "Machine learning ↔ LLM judge",
     "body": "Both find the unanticipated, differently: ML flags what is <em>unusual</em>, the judge flags what is <em>unwise</em>. A change can be one and not the other.",
     "accent": "info"},
    {"title": "Human ↔ everything else",
     "body": "The only verifier that knows why the system exists, and the only one you cannot buy more of. Spend it where the machines disagree.",
     "accent": "warning"},
  ], cols=3) }}
  {{ card_grid([
    {"title": "The deterministic one holds the gate",
     "body": "Blocking belongs to the verifier that returns the same verdict every time and can show its work to an auditor.",
     "accent": "success"},
    {"title": "The probabilistic ones advise",
     "body": "ML and LLM judges are excellent at raising a hand and terrible as the final word. A wrong comment is noise; a wrong block is an outage.",
     "accent": "secondary"},
    {"title": "Disagreement routes to a human",
     "body": "When the cheap verifiers disagree with each other, that is the signal worth a person&rsquo;s attention, rather than a fixed sampling rate.",
     "accent": "danger"},
  ], cols=3) }}
  {{ callout("If your only enforcement is a model, you do not have a control —
              you have a <strong>strong suggestion</strong>.", accent="warning") }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro ensembling_fast_verifiers() -%}
{#- The Weaver argument. The load-bearing assumption — verifiers must
    capture different, independent aspects of correctness — is stated on the
    slide, not left to the speaker notes, because without it the takeaway
    degrades to "add more cheap checks". The four kinds just demonstrated
    that independence concretely. -#}
{%- call content_slide("Ensembling verifiers") %}
  {{ slide_heading("",
                   "Ensembling multiple fast verifiers",
                   "Many small verifiers, each doing one thing well, beat one perfect judge.") }}
  {{ pros_cons(
       benefits=[
         {"title": "Fast, so they run everywhere",
          "detail": "Cheap enough for every commit, every tool call, every candidate answer."},
         {"title": "Naive, so they compose",
          "detail": "Each one checks a narrow slice extremely well. Narrowness is what makes combining them work."},
         {"title": "Diverse, so mistakes don&rsquo;t line up",
          "detail": "The gains assume each verifier captures a different, independent aspect of correctness. Redundant verifiers add nothing."},
       ],
       shortcomings=[
         {"title": "Oracle verifiers are perfect — and unavailable",
          "detail": "A verifier with perfect accuracy either doesn&rsquo;t scale (humans) or only covers a narrow domain (formal tools)."},
         {"title": "One big judge is a single point of failure",
          "detail": "Its blind spots are systematic. There is no second opinion to disagree with it."},
         {"title": "Bigger is slower and more expensive",
          "detail": "A verifier too costly to run on every change verifies a sample, not your delivery pipeline."},
       ],
       ben_label="Ensemble of fast, weak verifiers",
       short_label="One big oracle verifier") }}
  {{ callout("Weight the ensemble by each verifier&rsquo;s accuracy and the gap to a
              perfect verifier mostly closes — <strong>no oracle
              required</strong>.", accent="success") }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro weaver_evidence() -%}
{%- call content_slide("The evidence") %}
  {{ slide_heading("",
                   "Weighted ensembles outperform naive ones",
                   "Weaver, across four reasoning benchmarks.") }}
  <div style="flex:1; display:flex; flex-direction:column; justify-content:center; align-items:center; gap:14px;">
    <img src="modules/verifiers/img/weaver-weighted-vs-naive-ensembles.png"
         alt="Bar chart: improvement over a naive verifier ensemble on MATH500, GPQA Diamond, MMLU, and MMLU Pro. Supervised weighted ensembles (Naive Bayes, Logistic Regression) consistently beat oracle unweighted ensembles of top-1, top-5, and top-10 verifiers."
         class="plain"
         style="max-width:88%; max-height:52vh; border-radius:8px;"/>
    <p style="width:100%; text-align:right; font-size:11px; opacity:0.6; margin:0;">
      Figure from <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.18203">Saad-Falcon et al., &ldquo;Shrinking the Generation-Verification Gap with Weak Verifiers&rdquo; (2025)</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  {{ callout("An ensemble of small verifiers lifted a 70B open model to
              o3-mini-level accuracy — a jump that otherwise took a
              <strong>generation of post-training</strong>.", accent="info") }}
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}
